---

Can I apply to multiple open positions?<br><br>
You can, but we discourage it: if there are multiple open positions that seem plausible to you, you should determine the one that speaks most to you and focus your submission on that position. We try to write our job descriptions to help guide you, so if you are really struggling between two positions, carefully read the job descriptions to determine which is more apt.

How long do listings stay up?<br><br>
Listings will always stay up for at least two weeks, and many stay up longer. We ourselves don't know how many applicants we will have for a position (and we have been surprised in both directions, multiple times), so it's hard for us to give better forward visibility. We know that it takes some time to do materials, but if you see a listing up that you want to apply to, you should prioritize getting the materials done and submitted.

How much time should I spend on the materials?<br><br>
However long it takes you to accurately represent who you are and why you're interested in Oxide. The materials are the most important part of how we consider candidates, so give them enough time to do them well.

Can I use an LLM to help write my materials?<br><br>
We don't recommend it. LLMs can be effective editors — but they are much worse writers. In particular, if you (say) have an LLM write the answer to why you want to work for Oxide (and yes, people have done this!), you should really question whether you want to work for Oxide at all. Certainly, we will question whether you want to work for Oxide at all — and given how many people apply to Oxide, if we are questioning whether you actually want to work here, you are unlikely to be advanced to the next steps. So, treat an LLM as you might your mother: if you want your mother to review what you wrote for clarity and to offer her perspective, great — but if you're having your mother write your materials for you, please ask her to apply to Oxide herself instead.

Did I write too much?<br><br>
Unlikely! We deliberately ask open-ended questions that drive to the meaning that we all seek in our work; don't fret if you find that you have much to say!

I prepared materials, but now the listing seems to be gone; can I still submit them?<br><br>
Likely, yes. We de-list job descriptions for a period (e.g., a week) before we stop accepting submissions. If you find yourself in this situation and can't find the submission link, contact us.

What are the next steps after I submit my materials?<br><br>
If, based on your materials, we believe that there is a likely fit, we will contact you to schedule conversations with the Oxide team.

How long will it take to get a response to my application?<br><br>
All candidates will receive a response, but this takes time: multiple Oxide employees review every candidate and their materials. We process applications in the order received, and so the length of time may vary. 4-6 weeks is pretty normal, but it can be longer than that for positions that are particularly oversubscribed. We'll try to give you a sense of how long it will take when you apply. This is generally the longest part of the process.

Who reviews my materials?<br><br>
We don't have fixed hiring committees; any Oxide employee is eligible to participate in reviewing applications, and many do.

What's the interview process like?<br><br>
If we advance your application to conversations with the team, we'll ask you for some times that work well for you for a series of one-on-one conversations with Oxide employees, who will sign up to speak with you. Once the conversations are scheduled, we add a uniquely Oxide twist: Just as Oxide employees have your materials, we will also give you their materials ahead of time – allowing you to learn as much about the team as we are learning about you! We have found that this generally leads to much better conversations.

Do you hire people from outside of the US?<br><br>
We do, and we have employees who live in many different places. We do ask that your workday overlaps with Pacific Time for at least four hours.

What is the relative importance of the interview versus materials?<br><br>
Our hiring process is very much focused on the materials that candidates submit as part of their application. Our review of them constitutes the bulk of our process.

How many applicants does Oxide get?<br><br>
In a word: many. But this shouldn't be taken as discouragement; we have several employees at Oxide who felt themselves long shots.

Does a human review every application?<br><br>
It's unfortunate (though understandable!) that this needs to be asked, but yes, a human reviews (and in some cases, many humans review!) every application.

Does Oxide provide detailed feedback on rejected applications?<br><br>
Not really, no. As RFD 3 elaborates this is a consequence of our high degree of selectivity: there is often little constructive feedback we can offer. That said, you are welcome to contact us and we'll tell you what we can.

If Oxide rejects my application, can I reapply later?<br><br>
Yes – and we certainly encourage you to keep an eye on our openings, as they change over time, and a future opening may be a better fit for your background and interests.

Can I reapply immediately?<br><br>
Please don't. A human reviews all materials; if you reapply for a position for which you have been just rejected, you should expect the same result.

If I reapply in the future, will Oxide look at my previously submitted materials?<br><br>
Yes, we look at your previous materials not to reevaluate them, but rather to get to know you better: the change in materials over time (or frankly, lack thereof) can help us better understand your path and/or your fitness for a position other than the one for which you originally applied.